cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "dedekind"
abstract: >
  A symbolic-algebra library in standard C++23 that uses compile-time
  evaluation for static analysis, verification and optimisation. Its
  foundation is the Juliet Posture (Jlt), a fragment of C++23 under three
  rules: values are copyable data and functions are pure and constexpr;
  generic code varies with a type only through that type's concepts and
  traits; recursion is structural, on a type or on a natural number that
  counts down. On it the library builds an embedded, lazy language in
  layers: lattices of truth values from the Boolean and Kleene chains on
  (Pst); intensional sets, given by a membership test rather than a list
  (Lwv); a point-free relational calculus over cartesian products (Trsk);
  and symbolic algebra over the canonical sets (Ddk). Algebraic laws are
  stated as types and as constexpr values, so the compiler checks them and
  reduces expressions at build time, leaving what cannot fold as a
  specialised run-time residual. A checker in tools/jlt-check holds the core
  of the library to the three rules in continuous integration.
authors:
  - family-names: "Kräutler"
    given-names: "Vincent"
repository-code: "https://github.com/vincentk/dedekind"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
